  [PASS] 69d the mass scaling -- the only part of this item that could ever have been Kepler-grade -- is measured, and it LEANS AGAINST the framework without killing it.  sigma_los goes as M_b^(1/2) rather than the deep-MOND M^(1/4), i.e. sigma^2 proportional to M_b, the signature of a Newtonian-like law with a boosted G.  But it is only 3.0 sigma, and the distance control -- the same fit inside narrow velocity shells, where luminosity stops tracking distance -- pulls it back to +0.34 and is consistent with BOTH hypotheses.  Recorded as a hint against, not a kill   (d log sigma/d log M_b = +0.492 +/- 0.082 over 1.62 dex in M_b: 3.0 sigma from the isolated deep-MOND 1/4, 0.1 sigma from 1/2, 5.1 sigma from LambdaCDM's abundance-matched 0.91; velocity-shell mean +0.336)

  does the dispersion depend on how hard the isolation is pushed?  (if the excess over the framework is residual group contamination, tightening the isolation must bring it down)
    R_iso =    800 kpc: N = 2107, sigma_los =   190 +/-   5 km/s, A(deep-MOND) = 1.78 +/- 0.04 (+0.250 dex)
    R_iso =   1500 kpc: N =  976, sigma_los =   187 +/-   7 km/s, A(deep-MOND) = 1.74 +/- 0.06 (+0.242 dex)
    R_iso =   2500 kpc: N =  366, sigma_los =   200 +/-  10 km/s, A(deep-MOND) = 1.81 +/- 0.09 (+0.258 dex)
  and inside velocity shells -- the nearest shell is where 2MRS's isolation reaches furthest down the luminosity
  function, so if the amplitude excess were residual group contamination it should be smallest there:
    cz 3000-6500 km/s (N =  262, isolation complete to M_b > 1.4e+10 Msun): A(deep-MOND) = 1.51 +/- 0.12 (+0.180 dex)
    cz 6500-9000 km/s (N =  329, isolation complete to M_b > 3.3e+10 Msun): A(deep-MOND) = 1.66 +/- 0.10 (+0.221 dex)
    cz 9000-12000 km/s (N =  385, isolation complete to M_b > 5.8e+10 Msun): A(deep-MOND) = 1.99 +/- 0.11 (+0.300 dex)
    THE ONE THING THAT ARGUES FOR THE FRAMEWORK HERE: the excess shrinks as the isolation gets deeper (1.99 in the far shell -> 1.51 in the near one, +0.12 dex).  That is the signature of residual group contamination, and it says the 69c offset is an upper limit on the real one.
    It does not remove it: even the deepest-isolation shell needs A = 1.51 +/- 0.12, 5.2x the K-band baryonic mass, 5.4 sigma from 1.  A pair sample isolated against a catalogue two magnitudes deeper than 2MRS would settle it.

